crypto/tls: verify peer signatures by keys this package doesn't support

A peer whose leaf certificate has a public key that crypto/tls doesn't
support (x509.Certificate.PublicKey is nil), such as an ECDSA key on a
named curve that crypto/x509 doesn't implement, is rejected with an
unsupported_certificate alert before VerifyPeerCertificate and
VerifyConnection run. Applications can verify the certificate chain
themselves, but they have no way to verify the peer's handshake
signature.

Add Config.VerifyUnsupportedSignature. If it is set, such peers are
accepted in TLS 1.2 and TLS 1.3, and their ServerKeyExchange and
CertificateVerify signatures are passed to it. Before that, crypto/tls checks what it
checks for supported keys: the signature scheme must be one it
advertised, and in a ServerKeyExchange its signature algorithm must
match the cipher suite (an ECDSA scheme on an ECDHE_ECDSA suite, an RSA
scheme on an ECDHE_RSA suite). The alerts are unchanged: an invalid
ServerKeyExchange draws illegal_parameter from the client, a
CertificateVerify with a scheme that was not advertised draws
illegal_parameter, and a bad CertificateVerify signature draws
decrypt_error from the server. The hook is never called for supported
keys. Unset, behavior is unchanged. It is off in FIPS 140-3 mode.

In TLS 1.3, the signature schemes this package implements are bound to
public key types it supports (an ECDSA scheme to its curve), so a peer
with an unsupported key must sign with a scheme this package doesn't
implement, and that it advertised; others draw illegal_parameter, as
for a supported key that uses the wrong scheme. The function gets the
TLS 1.3 signed content (RFC 8446, Section 4.4.3). Such schemes are only
advertised by a later change that makes the list configurable; until
then, unsupported keys still fail in TLS 1.3.

The tests use a certificate whose key names a made-up curve OID but holds
a P-256 point, and a hook that verifies with crypto/ecdsa. Schemes that
were not advertised, or that don't match the suite, are produced by a
connection wrapper that rewrites the peer's handshake message.

ivory: P2, profiles core and kt. Base: go1.27.2 with P0 and P1.
Upstream: proposal B. Requirements: A_17124-03, A_22455, A_22890
(gemSpec_Krypt V2.51.0, gemSpec_KT V3.17.1).
Status: TLS 1.2 and TLS 1.3. The TLS 1.3 hook path
is tested end to end with P3 (opaque signers) and P5 (signature schemes).

